WEBVTT

00:14.480 --> 00:18.120
[theme song playing]

00:36.960 --> 00:38.920
[inaudible]

01:20.240 --> 01:24.160
[bell tolling in the distance]

01:24.240 --> 01:27.680
[men singing hymns
in the distance]

01:50.200 --> 01:54.240
You might think this is a curious place
for me to suggest a meeting.

01:54.320 --> 01:55.360
But,

01:56.520 --> 01:58.880
the surprises have only just begun.

01:58.960 --> 01:59.960
[door opens]

02:09.040 --> 02:11.320
You look a little
unsteady, Mr. Shelby.

02:11.400 --> 02:12.400
[door closes]

02:13.080 --> 02:14.320
Take a seat.

02:21.120 --> 02:22.640
You're working together.

02:24.240 --> 02:26.560
Blowing up the Garrison
was your idea.

02:27.680 --> 02:29.000
You who chose me...

02:33.280 --> 02:36.120
It's funny, I thought
the Fenians had a policy...

02:36.200 --> 02:37.000
Enough!

02:37.080 --> 02:39.600
...of not mixing the personal
with politics.

02:39.680 --> 02:43.000
- I said enough.
- I think I'll decide on what is enough.

02:43.080 --> 02:44.720
Who is in charge here then?

02:44.800 --> 02:47.160
Who is in charge?
Let me understand this.

02:47.480 --> 02:49.560
You answer to an agent
of the Crown?

02:49.640 --> 02:53.160
- Very interesting...
- Perhaps we can stick to the agenda.

02:53.240 --> 02:56.120
- Whose agenda?
- There is a form of words here...

02:56.200 --> 02:58.280
- Whose words?
- Arrogant bastard...

02:58.720 --> 03:01.480
I'm not accustomed to being
spoken to like that.

03:01.560 --> 03:02.720
Just come to order.

03:02.800 --> 03:05.680
I am here to listen,
not to the fucking infantry.

03:05.760 --> 03:06.720
Enough! Jesus.

03:07.360 --> 03:08.320
There...

03:11.200 --> 03:14.960
Some weeks ago, Mr. Shelby
carried out a military mission

03:15.040 --> 03:18.320
on behalf of pro-treaty Republicans
here in Birmingham--

03:18.400 --> 03:19.440
You...

03:19.520 --> 03:23.360
- Working for the King...
- There is a unique political situation.

03:23.440 --> 03:25.480
Yeah? How does that feel?

03:25.560 --> 03:26.920
- Let us--
- Tell me...

03:27.000 --> 03:31.080
- How does that fucking feel?
- Temporarily we have a shared interest.

03:31.160 --> 03:35.000
- Should I just stop him laughing?
- You want me to stop laughing?

03:35.080 --> 03:38.760
- Explain this, pal.
- Following the success of your mission...

03:38.840 --> 03:42.200
- We're going to make you do it again.
- Will you shut up?

03:42.280 --> 03:44.760
Can we just conduct
ourselves here, please?

03:44.840 --> 03:46.680
- Conduct ourselves...
- Please.

03:46.760 --> 03:50.560
No one in this room wants to breathe
the same air as anyone else.

03:51.600 --> 03:52.560
Nevertheless--

03:52.640 --> 03:56.920
Nevertheless, the pro-treaty paddies
and the King want the same man dead.

03:58.440 --> 03:59.720
Am I right?

04:22.360 --> 04:23.440
Why him?

04:23.520 --> 04:25.320
Did you ask 'why' in France?

04:25.880 --> 04:27.040
Yeah.

04:27.120 --> 04:29.400
So it's the same answer.

04:29.480 --> 04:30.800
Because...

04:37.480 --> 04:38.560
Why me?

04:39.760 --> 04:41.880
- Because...
- Because...

04:45.160 --> 04:48.520
Because if anyone connects me to this,
it will be assumed

04:48.600 --> 04:51.280
it was a criminal act
and not a political one.

04:51.360 --> 04:52.360
No.

04:52.440 --> 04:55.240
You need not fear being
connected to the killing

04:55.320 --> 04:58.800
because I will see to it
that no connection is ever made.

04:59.080 --> 05:01.960
You will have complete immunity
after the fact.

05:02.440 --> 05:06.560
But, in the execution, you will get
no help from agents of the Crown.

05:06.640 --> 05:08.760
And we can't be seen to be involved.

05:10.240 --> 05:12.400
You have been chosen, Mr. Shelby.

05:14.040 --> 05:17.400
- And there really is no choice.
- "No" equals bang.

05:17.840 --> 05:21.520
And your man here will shed
no tears as he looks the other way.

05:26.640 --> 05:30.240
Nevertheless, I say no.

05:30.320 --> 05:31.680
[door opens]

05:37.080 --> 05:38.400
[Campbell] Mr. Shelby?

05:44.560 --> 05:47.680
What about your
export license, Mr. Shelby?

05:47.760 --> 05:50.160
You had a deal with Mr. Churchill.

05:55.480 --> 05:58.040
One with the mouth,
Donal Henry, he's a spy.

05:58.120 --> 06:01.760
He's working for the irregulars
against the pro-treaty Paddies.

06:01.840 --> 06:04.320
He reports every fucking word
back to them.

06:04.400 --> 06:05.760
No, it's not possible.

06:05.840 --> 06:09.040
They've both been security
vetted by my own department.

06:09.120 --> 06:11.400
I have men in places
your men can't go.

06:11.480 --> 06:14.440
He meets the anti-treaty
Paddies in the back room

06:14.520 --> 06:17.840
of the Mother Red Cap in Saltley,
sometimes twice a week.

06:17.920 --> 06:21.040
These Irish enemies are
fucking brothers and cousins.

06:21.120 --> 06:24.080
You are stepping into a world
you don't understand.

06:27.080 --> 06:30.760
If what you're saying is true,
he'll be dealt with.

06:31.480 --> 06:34.560
Bring me proof
that the spy is dead

06:35.160 --> 06:36.680
and we have a deal.

06:52.880 --> 06:54.760
- Mr. Shelby.
- Billy boy.

06:54.840 --> 06:56.120
Watch it.

07:03.720 --> 07:05.160
Now, on you go.

07:19.520 --> 07:22.840
[dramatic music playing]

07:58.200 --> 08:01.200
Right, boys. Let's go!

08:02.120 --> 08:05.360
[people shouting and screaming]

08:34.560 --> 08:35.680
Fuck you!

08:36.800 --> 08:38.240
Mario!

08:39.920 --> 08:41.160
Mario!

08:44.160 --> 08:45.880
[woman screaming]

08:48.800 --> 08:50.600
- Mario!
- [glass shatters]

08:51.400 --> 08:52.480
Come here.

08:53.360 --> 08:54.960
[screaming]

08:57.120 --> 08:59.040
Sabini's day is done.

09:01.600 --> 09:03.120
I'll protect you now.

09:05.760 --> 09:07.400
Oh, shut up.

09:07.480 --> 09:09.840
Stop fucking whinging.

09:11.600 --> 09:13.920
Due to my razor blade

09:15.200 --> 09:18.040
and a few complaints
from the neighbors

09:18.240 --> 09:21.240
about the terrible
fucking music...

09:26.280 --> 09:28.440
Do you want to tell them
or should I?

09:29.320 --> 09:30.720
This place

09:30.800 --> 09:33.280
is under new management.

09:38.600 --> 09:41.120
By order of the Peaky Blinders.

09:44.240 --> 09:45.880
[upbeat music playing]

09:51.840 --> 09:53.640
[whooping]

09:57.040 --> 09:58.160
[laughs]

10:14.000 --> 10:15.320
[gasps]

10:35.800 --> 10:37.120
[yawns]

10:42.120 --> 10:43.640
[clears throat]

11:14.400 --> 11:15.720
[clears throat]

11:26.920 --> 11:28.960
- [clears throat]
- [knock at door]

11:29.040 --> 11:30.120
Yes?

11:31.480 --> 11:33.360
Somebody to see you,
Mr. Shelby.

11:33.440 --> 11:36.640
He says he's come about
the position of accounts clerk.

11:36.720 --> 11:37.600
Send him in.

11:37.680 --> 11:39.720
[footsteps retreating]

11:41.360 --> 11:44.560
- [Lizzie] Just through here, sir...
- [man] Thank you.

11:44.640 --> 11:46.520
[footsteps approaching]

11:48.080 --> 11:49.280
Michael...

11:51.880 --> 11:53.560
Vacancy's been filled.

11:54.960 --> 11:56.000
[clears throat]

11:56.080 --> 11:58.320
Not according to
this morning's paper.

12:06.200 --> 12:09.000
At school I was top of the class
in mathematics.

12:09.080 --> 12:13.040
And I did a night school course
in accountancy at Worcester College.

12:14.480 --> 12:18.880
Mum says you've had six different
accountant clerks in the last six months.

12:19.880 --> 12:23.840
When respectable men see
the other things you do, they leave.

12:25.320 --> 12:28.760
Well, I, like Lizzie out there,
already know what you do.

12:31.440 --> 12:34.800
Mum says you want to be 80 percent
legal within two years.

12:34.880 --> 12:37.080
- Three.
- I can help.

12:38.760 --> 12:42.280
I know both sides and you know
you can trust me.

12:43.800 --> 12:45.400
I want to help you, Tommy,

12:46.160 --> 12:47.360
become legal.

12:50.040 --> 12:51.600
Do the right thing.

12:55.920 --> 12:57.960
I want to be proud of this family.

13:04.960 --> 13:07.200
Does Polly know you're here, Michael?

13:09.120 --> 13:11.400
I'm 18 on Friday. I go where I please.

13:27.360 --> 13:28.920
You phone your mother.

13:31.920 --> 13:33.160
Good luck.

13:40.480 --> 13:42.200
[loud knocking at door]

13:42.280 --> 13:44.200
We're not open for winnings yet.

13:44.280 --> 13:46.840
[woman]
I'm here to see Thomas Shelby. 

13:49.080 --> 13:51.040
[children chattering excitedly]

13:51.840 --> 13:53.240
I have an appointment.

13:54.040 --> 13:55.240
Come in.

14:08.520 --> 14:09.720
May Carleton.

14:09.800 --> 14:13.000
Mr. Shelby has his proper
meetings in his other office.

14:14.400 --> 14:16.840
This was the address
that he gave me.

14:16.920 --> 14:20.400
- He gave you this address?
- He said it was a gambling den.

14:20.480 --> 14:23.040
I told him that I'd
always wanted to see one

14:23.120 --> 14:26.120
since I am always reading
about them in the papers.

14:26.200 --> 14:28.880
Quite the big scandal in
the Telegraph lately.

14:28.960 --> 14:31.600
Anyway, he said I could come
and have a look.

14:33.600 --> 14:37.400
And now I feel a bit of an idiot
because I'm early and he's late.

14:37.480 --> 14:40.720
- And I have no idea how to behave.
- In a gambling den?

14:42.400 --> 14:43.800
I'll wait in the car.

14:45.200 --> 14:48.320
If I open the door again,
they'll all want to come in.

14:48.960 --> 14:50.200
Like flies.

14:55.360 --> 14:56.720
So what are you?

14:57.640 --> 14:59.200
"What" am I?

14:59.280 --> 15:00.400
To Thomas?

15:02.600 --> 15:04.920
I'm going to train his racehorse.

15:05.360 --> 15:06.520
For Epsom.

15:07.280 --> 15:08.520
You know horses?

15:09.880 --> 15:10.920
Yes.

15:11.320 --> 15:12.280
You?

15:12.640 --> 15:13.960
Born riding.

15:15.400 --> 15:17.680
I slept in a manger
when I was a baby.

15:19.720 --> 15:21.400
I was born riding too.

15:24.760 --> 15:25.840
[door slams shut]

15:25.920 --> 15:30.080
There's a fucking great Riley parked
out there and nobody's watching it.

15:30.160 --> 15:33.160
John, this woman says
she's training Thomas' horse.

15:37.040 --> 15:39.040
He's told us a lot about you.

15:41.040 --> 15:43.120
Don't know where he is, do you?

15:45.320 --> 15:47.200
Sorry, I am late.
[clears throat]

15:47.280 --> 15:48.800
There was
a family matter.

15:48.880 --> 15:52.000
- Esme, keep it locked up for a minute.
- Yes, Thomas.

15:52.080 --> 15:53.360
I'll show you around.

16:01.440 --> 16:04.520
So, be my guest.

16:14.760 --> 16:16.760
That's my brother
Arthur's office.

16:21.320 --> 16:23.560
Down there is my brother
John's office.

16:23.640 --> 16:26.480
That's where we slate
the runners and the riders.

16:29.200 --> 16:32.200
It's so out in the open.
What about the police?

16:33.320 --> 16:34.800
What about them?

16:36.280 --> 16:40.320
When I drove into Small Heath,
I thought I was going to get murdered.

16:40.400 --> 16:42.240
Then I mentioned your name.

16:43.800 --> 16:46.200
It was like being escorted
to see a king.

16:49.360 --> 16:52.080
- You came here to get my girl, right?
- Right.

17:06.520 --> 17:09.080
She looks in very good
condition considering

17:09.160 --> 17:11.160
she's not been out on the gallops.

17:11.240 --> 17:13.440
Curly is the best horseman
in England.

17:13.520 --> 17:16.320
I have a man called Micky
who'd argue the point.

17:16.400 --> 17:18.760
Ah, but Curly here
is half horse himself.

17:18.840 --> 17:20.560
She's blessed. Very blessed.

17:20.640 --> 17:22.520
Well, she's from the best stock.

17:22.600 --> 17:25.840
I'd rather have a colored pony.
Mixed blood is stronger.

17:25.920 --> 17:28.760
- Not to win the Derby.
- Don't like them racing.

17:28.840 --> 17:30.480
Not the way they beat them.

17:30.560 --> 17:32.760
- She's all yours.
- Thank you.

17:36.640 --> 17:39.040
[Charlie] He gets sad
when one leaves.

17:39.120 --> 17:41.200
So what time is
your box van coming?

17:41.280 --> 17:42.440
Midday.

17:42.520 --> 17:45.480
You've got time to take the lady
to the Garrison.

17:45.560 --> 17:47.760
Show her the spa
and the tea rooms.

17:49.720 --> 17:51.400
I'd like that.
Why not?

17:54.400 --> 17:55.760
[clears throat]

17:58.040 --> 18:00.440
I just had it done up.
There was a fire.

18:01.680 --> 18:02.880
It's...

18:02.960 --> 18:04.080
Yep.

18:05.840 --> 18:06.960
What do you drink?

18:08.360 --> 18:10.080
It's so early, but gin.

18:27.320 --> 18:28.840
Goodness. With something?

18:31.440 --> 18:32.640
Like what?

18:34.680 --> 18:36.120
Tonic water or...

18:37.640 --> 18:39.080
Uh... [clears throat]

18:39.160 --> 18:42.080
We have, um...cordial.

18:44.440 --> 18:46.640
There's not much room
in there, but...

18:50.440 --> 18:51.680
[sighs]

18:57.160 --> 18:59.240
You want to fuck me, Mrs. Carleton?

19:01.680 --> 19:02.880
Perhaps because I...

19:04.080 --> 19:06.680
Perhaps because I represent
something to you?

19:08.640 --> 19:11.520
We should have this conversation
before the booze

19:12.000 --> 19:13.560
starts talking for us.

19:14.240 --> 19:15.520
[lights match]

19:16.560 --> 19:17.960
You have a horse...

19:20.360 --> 19:21.680
Yes, I have a horse.

19:22.920 --> 19:25.040
The horse is why I'm here.

19:26.680 --> 19:27.840
Purely that.

19:28.680 --> 19:30.360
And because you
are paying me

19:30.440 --> 19:33.720
a lot of money to train your horse.
That's why I'm here.

19:34.080 --> 19:35.240
Purely that.

19:38.000 --> 19:39.040
Mm.

19:39.120 --> 19:40.120
Good.

19:41.920 --> 19:44.000
Good! Well, a toast, then.

19:44.440 --> 19:46.800
To the horse, to the Derby.

19:50.640 --> 19:52.440
- Cheers.
- Cheers.

19:57.760 --> 19:59.760
So is that what you say to women?

20:02.720 --> 20:05.240
I've only done that with them once.

20:05.400 --> 20:07.560
And what if they don't want to fuck?

20:08.840 --> 20:10.240
Then life is simpler.

20:11.280 --> 20:12.840
You want a simple life?

20:13.840 --> 20:15.000
[scoffs]

20:16.440 --> 20:19.360
Do I look like a man
who wants a simple life, eh?

20:20.480 --> 20:22.080
So what do you want?

20:24.520 --> 20:26.760
For what I'm paying you,
Mrs. Carleton,

20:27.520 --> 20:31.320
I want a horse that'll pay out
at Epsom on an each way bet.

20:37.160 --> 20:38.880
Before I took your account...

20:38.960 --> 20:40.120
[clears throat]

20:40.640 --> 20:42.560
I did some credit checks on you.

20:44.040 --> 20:46.680
Apparently you don't exist.

20:52.440 --> 20:54.440
My existence is questionable.

20:57.280 --> 20:59.040
Gypsies don't like registers.

21:00.480 --> 21:03.400
You see I'm not a Gypsy
like you mean.

21:04.320 --> 21:06.240
But you did register for France.

21:08.560 --> 21:10.920
I also sent your name to the war office.

21:11.000 --> 21:12.560
I have friends there.

21:13.520 --> 21:15.720
You won two medals
for gallantry.

21:17.720 --> 21:21.000
Does that really impress you,
Mrs. Carleton?

21:25.640 --> 21:26.920
Call me May.

21:28.640 --> 21:29.880
[door opens]

21:32.000 --> 21:33.360
[door closes]

21:33.760 --> 21:35.680
Charlie says
the box van is here.

21:36.840 --> 21:38.280
Thank you, Finn.

21:39.480 --> 21:40.600
[door opens]

21:41.680 --> 21:45.520
You know, I still don't know
what you're going to call your horse.

21:46.840 --> 21:49.920
The horse will be called
Grace's Secret.

21:52.520 --> 21:53.960
Grace's Secret.

22:00.400 --> 22:02.960
I told him he definitely
can't take the job.

22:04.720 --> 22:07.480
He said, "All right,
I'll move to London then."

22:07.560 --> 22:09.640
I said I won't give in
to blackmail.

22:10.080 --> 22:11.440
No is no.

22:12.720 --> 22:15.800
Well, that settles it then.

22:17.120 --> 22:19.480
I'll give the job to
the other candidate.

22:20.040 --> 22:22.040
He is much better
qualified anyway.

22:22.120 --> 22:23.920
Michael did City and Guilds.

22:24.000 --> 22:25.280
Six months.

22:25.360 --> 22:27.600
Yeah. But he doesn't
know racing, Pol.

22:28.640 --> 22:30.200
He knows horses

22:30.440 --> 22:32.120
but he doesn't know racing.

22:32.400 --> 22:34.440
This other boy,
he knows racing.

22:34.520 --> 22:36.600
He's good. He is a fast learner.

22:40.000 --> 22:41.800
What if Michael really leaves?

22:43.080 --> 22:45.280
You've just got to let him go, Pol.

22:45.360 --> 22:47.720
He will get a job
in an office in London.

22:48.160 --> 22:49.840
I'm sure he'll write to you.

22:50.680 --> 22:52.200
He made a big speech.

22:52.280 --> 22:55.640
About wanting to help you build up
a respectable business.

22:56.680 --> 22:58.880
He's like his dad.
He can make me cry.

22:58.960 --> 23:00.600
There are trains to London.

23:01.920 --> 23:03.640
So I just let him leave?

23:03.720 --> 23:05.720
Every month or so he'd be back.

23:07.000 --> 23:09.120
Unless of course he meets a girl...

23:09.440 --> 23:11.200
Then who knows?

23:16.720 --> 23:18.080
Oh, my God.

23:19.880 --> 23:21.560
You want him to do this job.

23:21.640 --> 23:24.680
Polly, I want what you want.

23:26.320 --> 23:30.000
What about this other
more qualified candidate?

23:30.640 --> 23:31.840
Hm.

23:32.720 --> 23:34.560
Well, I lied. There isn't one.

23:39.200 --> 23:40.640
How much are you paying?

23:42.360 --> 23:44.720
- The advertised rate.
- Like hell.

23:45.240 --> 23:47.400
- Three bob.
- Like hell.

23:48.440 --> 23:49.960
- Three and six.
- Four.

23:51.520 --> 23:53.000
Four it is.

23:58.600 --> 24:00.760
- I love him, Tom.
- I know.

24:00.840 --> 24:04.040
That's why I'll keep him away
from the old business.

24:04.120 --> 24:05.960
And put him charge of the new.

24:06.040 --> 24:07.200
Agreed?

24:07.880 --> 24:09.160
Agreed.

24:18.640 --> 24:21.640
Well, Finn, you have got
two choices, mild or mild.

24:22.440 --> 24:24.720
- Mild.
- Here you go.

24:24.800 --> 24:26.960
- [door opens]
- There he is. Look.

24:29.040 --> 24:31.200
[all cheering]

24:31.280 --> 24:32.720
Happy birthday, Michael.

24:32.800 --> 24:35.040
Eighteen years old.
You're a man today.

24:35.120 --> 24:37.760
- Give him a drink, John boy.
- There you go.

24:37.840 --> 24:41.320
And after that, we will go
and find you a lady of the night.

24:41.400 --> 24:42.520
Arthur!

24:46.280 --> 24:48.040
Michael... [clears throat]

24:48.400 --> 24:49.600
What is that?

24:50.640 --> 24:52.960
[Thomas]
So you are never late for work.

24:56.560 --> 24:59.080
[John] Welcome to
the business, Michael.

24:59.160 --> 25:01.520
- Welcome to the business.
- Thank you.

25:01.600 --> 25:04.640
Right, come on, let's get him drunk.
Come here, you.

25:04.720 --> 25:06.760
- All right. Whiskey.
- No whiskey.

25:06.840 --> 25:09.640
He's got work tomorrow.
Give him only dark mild.

25:09.840 --> 25:11.280
A toast...

25:11.360 --> 25:12.800
- A toast.
- To Michael.

25:12.880 --> 25:14.440
- To Michael!
- To Michael!

25:26.400 --> 25:27.480
[door opens]

25:31.880 --> 25:33.080
You all right?

25:34.880 --> 25:36.200
[clears throat]

25:41.800 --> 25:44.360
- What is it? Just us?
- Just us.

25:44.440 --> 25:46.040
[Polly] Are we celebrating?

25:47.360 --> 25:48.520
Just taste this.

26:03.840 --> 26:05.400
What do you think, Arthur?

26:05.480 --> 26:08.920
Yeah, it is good, good stuff, really nice.

26:09.000 --> 26:10.600
Too good for the Garrison.

26:10.680 --> 26:14.040
I suppose we could shift it to
the toffs at the Eden club.

26:14.960 --> 26:16.760
Why? What is it?

26:16.840 --> 26:18.800
That is part of an export drive.

26:20.520 --> 26:23.640
We now have our secure warehouse
in Camden Town

26:23.720 --> 26:26.360
and secure passage to
the Poplar docks.

26:26.440 --> 26:28.280
So on Monday morning

26:28.360 --> 26:31.000
we'll be sending out
our first export crate.

26:31.080 --> 26:34.080
A crate of Riley car spares

26:34.960 --> 26:37.400
bound for Halifax, Nova Scotia.

26:37.960 --> 26:39.680
Where there is prohibition?

26:40.760 --> 26:42.440
Where there is prohibition.

26:43.440 --> 26:45.200
All over Canada and America

26:45.280 --> 26:47.240
people are making
their own booze.

26:47.320 --> 26:48.800
In bath tubs.

26:48.880 --> 26:51.640
But rich people in New York,

26:51.720 --> 26:54.080
in Toronto and Boston

26:54.160 --> 26:57.200
are still paying a lot of money
for the real stuff.

26:57.280 --> 27:00.560
So on Monday, the first
Shelby Company crate

27:00.640 --> 27:04.280
will contain 1000 Riley carburetors

27:04.360 --> 27:06.200
but hidden in the packing,

27:06.280 --> 27:08.280
will be 500 bottles

27:08.360 --> 27:10.320
of the finest quality

27:10.400 --> 27:13.080
single malt Scotch whiskey.

27:16.040 --> 27:17.880
We Shelbys have a license

27:17.960 --> 27:20.640
granted by the Minister
of the Empire himself,

27:20.720 --> 27:23.080
which means our crates
won't be searched.

27:23.160 --> 27:26.640
And, Polly, all of the whiskey
will be packed at the docks

27:26.720 --> 27:30.600
so Michael can do the books
without being involved.

27:30.680 --> 27:33.600
Like I've been telling you
all for a year now,

27:33.680 --> 27:35.560
motor cars are the future.

27:37.040 --> 27:38.680
[men speaking indistinctly]

27:39.520 --> 27:42.360
So, how's your life been, Tom?

27:42.440 --> 27:44.920
On the up, Johnny. On the up.

27:45.000 --> 27:48.440
Yeah, but, Tom, really, come on.
How is it?

27:48.520 --> 27:51.800
You know I hate to see you
not even married yet.

27:51.880 --> 27:55.400
I have a fine-looking cousin,
she would make your life hell.

27:55.480 --> 27:56.480
You deserve her.

27:56.560 --> 27:57.560
[laughs]

27:59.120 --> 28:02.040
We haven't had a good old
wedding in a long time.

28:04.040 --> 28:07.040
Have you had a look inside
these boxes then, Johnny?

28:07.120 --> 28:09.800
Now why do I want to look
at car parts, Tommy?

28:09.880 --> 28:11.680
When I haven't even got a car.

28:13.520 --> 28:15.680
Faith in family is a fine thing, eh?

28:16.800 --> 28:18.560
And I won't even be counting.

28:19.560 --> 28:22.520
If 25 becomes 24,
then 24 it is.

28:22.600 --> 28:24.920
You know I am no good
with numbers, Tom.

28:26.360 --> 28:29.800
But if 24 ever became 23,

28:30.480 --> 28:32.000
then that would be tax.

28:33.000 --> 28:34.400
And we don't pay tax.

28:35.560 --> 28:36.880
No, Tom.

28:38.720 --> 28:40.000
Good man!

28:40.120 --> 28:41.840
[upbeat music playing]

28:49.760 --> 28:51.240
I put an iron door on.

28:51.320 --> 28:54.880
And we put iron bars on the windows
and across the skylights.

28:54.960 --> 28:56.040
Good.

28:56.120 --> 28:58.640
So what will you be
keeping in here, Tommy?

28:58.720 --> 29:01.520
Temptation, Billy. Temptation.

29:06.720 --> 29:08.880
[moaning]

29:12.360 --> 29:13.560
[door opens loudly]

29:15.040 --> 29:17.440
Morning, Arthur.
I was just passing.

29:22.760 --> 29:24.960
[Arthur]
I think I am in fucking love.

29:25.840 --> 29:26.880
Drink!

29:27.520 --> 29:28.760
[exhales]

29:32.760 --> 29:34.520
She don't know where to look.

29:36.000 --> 29:37.080
Whiskey, Tommy?

29:38.280 --> 29:41.160
- No. I've got a long drive ahead.
- You off home?

29:42.080 --> 29:43.440
Mm-hmm.

29:43.520 --> 29:44.680
Eventually.

29:45.440 --> 29:46.680
To Birmingham.

29:52.320 --> 29:53.600
What's this?

29:55.800 --> 29:56.880
Olives.

29:56.960 --> 29:58.880
Yes, that's miscellaneous.

29:59.480 --> 30:00.600
Um...

30:00.680 --> 30:02.720
Olives. On sticks.

30:02.800 --> 30:06.240
You know, with little bits
of fucking onion and things.

30:08.240 --> 30:09.720
That's what it is.

30:11.520 --> 30:13.920
We've taken 600 pounds
on olives.

30:14.720 --> 30:16.760
With little bits of onion.

30:24.720 --> 30:26.240
I told you, Arthur.

30:28.560 --> 30:32.440
The dealers sell the cocaine
and we take a cut.

30:32.520 --> 30:34.560
We don't sell it direct.

30:34.640 --> 30:36.720
The Home Secretary
is cracking down

30:36.800 --> 30:41.200
and I don't want this to fuck up
everything else. Understand?

30:42.640 --> 30:44.720
I understand.

30:44.800 --> 30:47.520
How much of that 600 came out
of your pocket?

30:55.040 --> 30:56.480
It's under control.

30:56.560 --> 31:00.160
I put you down here because people
are scared of you, Arthur.

31:02.040 --> 31:03.920
But if you don't straighten up,

31:04.000 --> 31:05.840
it'll be John's turn in London.

31:06.960 --> 31:10.480
No need, I can handle it.

31:11.960 --> 31:13.360
It's under control?

31:14.640 --> 31:16.080
It's under control.

31:25.880 --> 31:27.800
Fucking tidy profit, though, eh?

31:31.880 --> 31:33.160
It's happening, Tom.

31:36.200 --> 31:37.600
Good.

31:38.600 --> 31:39.840
Good.

31:44.920 --> 31:46.640
Straighten up, soldier.

31:54.640 --> 31:56.000
[door closes]

31:59.400 --> 32:01.400
Yes, sir, Sergeant Major!

32:06.360 --> 32:07.720
[turns off ignition]

32:23.760 --> 32:25.080
[clears throat]

32:30.600 --> 32:31.840
What's your business?

32:33.520 --> 32:35.400
Sorry, I'm lost.

32:37.320 --> 32:39.760
Yeah, well, you can't stop here.
Move on.

32:41.560 --> 32:43.280
[starts ignition]

32:50.080 --> 32:51.720
Twenty-three seconds.

33:01.240 --> 33:03.880
It's all right, Ada,
it'll be Andrew for me.

33:07.800 --> 33:08.960
Hello!

33:09.920 --> 33:11.400
I'm looking for Ada.

33:11.760 --> 33:12.960
Who are you?

33:20.520 --> 33:22.480
- [door closes]
- Ada...

33:22.560 --> 33:24.040
I asked you a question.

33:24.120 --> 33:25.600
It's all right, James.

33:25.880 --> 33:27.160
This is my brother.

33:28.280 --> 33:29.520
Who's he?

33:29.600 --> 33:32.640
- He rents a room.
- You need to rent out rooms?

33:32.720 --> 33:34.880
Actually, she doesn't charge rent.

33:34.960 --> 33:37.520
He's a writer.
Which means he's skint.

33:40.240 --> 33:42.160
You get up late these days, Ada.

33:42.240 --> 33:43.440
I go to bed late.

33:43.520 --> 33:44.800
Where's Karl?

33:45.440 --> 33:46.880
What do you want, Tommy?

33:49.240 --> 33:52.680
Oh, God, before you start sizing
him up for a wedding suit,

33:52.760 --> 33:55.720
he's not interested in me.
Or in girls of any kind.

33:55.800 --> 33:56.840
- Ada?
- What?

33:56.920 --> 34:00.560
Tommy won't judge you.
He sure as hell won't go to the police.

34:01.760 --> 34:03.600
Look, I'll go and get dressed.

34:04.040 --> 34:06.600
James. I'm Thomas.

34:07.680 --> 34:09.040
Pleased to meet you.

34:12.640 --> 34:15.240
Could I have a minute
with my sister, please?

34:16.560 --> 34:17.800
Yes, of course.

34:21.840 --> 34:23.080
[clears throat]

34:26.440 --> 34:28.960
So, does your lodger know
your name?

34:29.120 --> 34:31.040
Yeah. Thorne.

34:31.120 --> 34:33.560
You think I'd tell anybody anything else?

34:33.640 --> 34:36.560
Your Brummie boys
are all over the papers.

34:36.640 --> 34:39.240
Just one last push, eh?
Then you'll go legit.

34:39.320 --> 34:43.080
Just one more obstacle to get round.
Then it'll all be straight.

34:43.160 --> 34:44.720
- Actually, yes!
- [scoffs]

34:45.760 --> 34:47.920
Personally, I find it
quite amusing.

34:48.000 --> 34:51.080
Men like you are becoming
very fashionable down here.

34:51.160 --> 34:53.560
No society party in London is complete

34:53.640 --> 34:56.520
without a gangster
for the girls to go giddy for.

34:57.800 --> 35:00.280
Anyway, what is it
that you want?

35:02.200 --> 35:04.240
I don't have any children, Ada.

35:06.720 --> 35:09.480
So I have set up a trust fund.

35:10.880 --> 35:14.360
The beneficiaries will be
John's kids and Karl.

35:15.600 --> 35:17.400
In order for Karl to benefit,

35:17.480 --> 35:19.800
I need your signature.

35:22.800 --> 35:25.800
I've set up an account.

35:25.880 --> 35:28.120
And money will be transferred

35:28.760 --> 35:30.600
in the event of my death.

35:32.160 --> 35:34.520
It will set them up
for a new life.

35:41.840 --> 35:43.360
Are you sick?

35:43.720 --> 35:46.320
I'm just doing what
any ordinary man would.

35:46.400 --> 35:48.240
Putting my affairs in order.

36:16.960 --> 36:19.920
Did you know they were going
to take the Eden club?

36:20.000 --> 36:22.440
I know about the Eden club. Yes.

36:22.520 --> 36:25.880
Did you know they were going to
do it before they did it?

36:25.960 --> 36:28.880
Because that's the one who
is in charge, isn't it?

36:28.960 --> 36:31.280
The one who knows before it happens.

36:31.360 --> 36:33.200
I know what I know.

36:33.280 --> 36:34.880
If you don't know,

36:34.960 --> 36:38.480
then you don't fucking know, do you?

36:38.560 --> 36:41.680
Did you know they was going to
take Wimbledon dogs?

36:41.760 --> 36:44.720
- And Harringay? And the trotting tracks?
- Uh...

36:46.160 --> 36:47.640
Talk, all right?

36:47.720 --> 36:50.800
I genuinely want to hear you.

36:50.880 --> 36:53.120
- You've lost control, Alfie.
- Have I?

36:53.720 --> 36:55.240
Everybody knows.

36:57.760 --> 37:00.080
- Talk to me, Alfie.
- Talk to you?

37:02.000 --> 37:03.800
All right, I will talk to you.

37:03.880 --> 37:07.880
I will fucking tell you how it was, right?
What happened was this...

37:07.960 --> 37:11.160
All the time you were talking
about dirty fucking Jews.

37:11.240 --> 37:12.640
All the fucking time...

37:12.720 --> 37:15.760
Dirty fucking Jews, dirty kikes,
dirty dustbin lids.

37:15.840 --> 37:18.480
You wanna talk about
not getting into places?

37:18.560 --> 37:20.360
Everybody makes fucking jokes.

37:20.440 --> 37:22.760
I didn't know you had
no sense of humor.

37:22.840 --> 37:25.040
And you were pushing
everybody around.

37:25.120 --> 37:27.240
And I am not going
to fucking mention

37:27.320 --> 37:30.600
the great big fuck off
elephant in the room.

37:32.160 --> 37:33.360
Look, Alfie...

37:33.440 --> 37:37.400
How many of my bookies
worked Epsom this last May?

37:37.480 --> 37:39.520
None. None.

37:39.600 --> 37:41.120
Not one.

37:41.200 --> 37:44.960
So, for a few fucking jokes and Epsom,

37:45.040 --> 37:48.560
you fucking invite a tribe
of savages into the city

37:48.640 --> 37:51.560
and just fucking clog
the fucking gates!

37:56.520 --> 37:59.160
I'm sorry, what are you saying, mate?

37:59.240 --> 38:00.520
I'm saying

38:02.120 --> 38:04.800
I am sorry about the Jew thing.

38:05.480 --> 38:08.360
- Are you saying you are sorry?
- Yeah.

38:08.680 --> 38:11.280
- You are sorry about the Jew thing?
- Yeah.

38:11.600 --> 38:13.520
- Really?
- Yeah.

38:14.000 --> 38:17.440
I want that in writing.
Write that down. He said I'm sorry.

38:17.920 --> 38:19.440
[man starts writing]

38:19.520 --> 38:21.040
And also write down

38:21.120 --> 38:23.840
that all our bookies
can go back to Epsom...

38:23.920 --> 38:26.440
- He didn't say that.
- Who's asking you?

38:28.000 --> 38:29.480
Write it down.

38:29.760 --> 38:31.200
It's all right. Come on.

38:32.520 --> 38:34.160
Alfie is an old friend.

38:36.360 --> 38:37.800
My friend.

38:39.160 --> 38:40.840
Now. Admit it.

38:41.520 --> 38:43.200
The Peaky Blinders

38:43.280 --> 38:44.720
is out of control.

38:44.800 --> 38:47.320
Yeah. They are out
of fucking control, mate.

38:47.400 --> 38:50.840
They came down the canal
and spread like the fucking clap.

38:51.440 --> 38:52.280
Right.

38:52.360 --> 38:55.040
- So now they are everybody's problem.
- Yeah.

38:55.480 --> 38:57.040
- Alfie.
- Mm.

38:57.160 --> 39:00.480
You and me, we've been fighting
since we were at school.

39:00.560 --> 39:02.120
- Yeah.
- All right.

39:02.200 --> 39:05.640
- Now also, we've been friends.
- Yeah.

39:05.720 --> 39:08.360
You know it goes
backwards and forwards.

39:09.280 --> 39:11.680
How much better is it
when we're friends?

39:11.760 --> 39:13.720
Oh, it's much better, mate. Yeah.

39:13.800 --> 39:15.320
You write that down.

39:15.400 --> 39:16.320
Mm.

39:17.640 --> 39:20.400
So what we are doing here is
we are writing down

39:20.480 --> 39:22.440
some kind of a deal thing, right?

39:22.520 --> 39:24.960
- Yeah. A written deal.
- A written deal.

39:25.040 --> 39:27.320
Good, then why don't we discuss

39:27.400 --> 39:30.000
the border between the Italians

39:30.080 --> 39:32.600
and the Jews going back
to Farringdon Road?

39:32.680 --> 39:33.840
Farrington Road--

39:33.920 --> 39:37.240
- Yeah. Farringdon Road.
- Fuck. That is ancient history.

39:37.320 --> 39:38.880
Write down
Farrington Road.

39:38.960 --> 39:42.000
- No, no. Write down Camden Road.
- Farringdon Road.

39:42.080 --> 39:44.200
- Farringdon Road...
- Camden Road...

39:44.280 --> 39:45.960
Write down Camden Road.

39:47.120 --> 39:49.080
Just write down peace.

39:49.160 --> 39:51.000
Just write down peace

39:51.080 --> 39:55.520
between the Jews and the Italians.

39:56.720 --> 40:00.520
And war against the Gypsies.

40:02.440 --> 40:03.800
Hm.

40:24.680 --> 40:26.040
Fuck.

40:31.400 --> 40:33.880
- [door opens]
- Mr. Thomas Shelby, madam.

40:40.720 --> 40:42.080
[door closes]

40:48.240 --> 40:49.360
Nice house.

40:53.680 --> 40:55.160
Would you like a whiskey?

40:55.720 --> 40:56.920
Scotch or Irish.

40:57.600 --> 40:58.640
Irish.

41:05.560 --> 41:09.520
Sorry for the short notice.
I was passing.

41:10.240 --> 41:12.520
We're not quite on
the Birmingham Road.

41:12.600 --> 41:14.320
A small diversion.

41:14.400 --> 41:16.040
Sixty miles. I checked.

41:17.200 --> 41:19.320
That's a small diversion
these days.

41:20.680 --> 41:22.400
So I am a small diversion.

41:23.520 --> 41:24.480
Good.

41:27.040 --> 41:29.280
Your horse is still out
on the gallops.

41:29.360 --> 41:31.480
But I had Micky
prepare this report.

41:31.560 --> 41:35.000
You can read it now if you like,
then we'll go and see her.

41:41.920 --> 41:42.840
[clears throat]

41:42.920 --> 41:46.320
- Is there enough light for you to read?
- Yes, it's fine.

41:48.360 --> 41:50.120
It's getting quite late.

41:59.560 --> 42:02.120
Micky says she has the lungs
of an elephant.

42:03.000 --> 42:07.040
She is nimble in the heavy going
but bounces a little on hard ground.

42:07.400 --> 42:08.920
Sorry, I'll let you read.

42:11.720 --> 42:15.000
So he thinks she will favor good
to soft ground, eh?

42:15.240 --> 42:17.320
- A little juice in the grass.
- Mm.

42:17.960 --> 42:19.760
Well, that is good for Epsom.

42:21.960 --> 42:24.640
Does he think she's not
good enough for Epsom?

42:25.600 --> 42:27.040
She needs a lot of work.

42:28.120 --> 42:30.280
The race board will need convincing.

42:31.000 --> 42:33.000
I imagine you have some influence.

42:33.520 --> 42:34.760
Hm.

42:35.720 --> 42:38.760
That's the suggested program
for the next two months.

42:42.160 --> 42:43.640
Your glass is empty.

42:43.760 --> 42:45.280
Yes, it's fine.

42:47.800 --> 42:49.240
Are you hungry?

42:50.480 --> 42:51.960
Yes. A bit.

42:52.800 --> 42:54.240
I can prepare something.

42:55.560 --> 42:57.240
Do you have engine oil?

43:00.120 --> 43:03.080
Before I set off for Birmingham,
the car needs oil.

43:04.800 --> 43:08.920
I'm sure my husband kept some
in the garage. I'll get someone to do it.

43:09.200 --> 43:10.920
This evening or tomorrow?

43:15.160 --> 43:18.280
We are so far from anywhere,
people usually stay over.

43:20.080 --> 43:24.520
Especially before cars and carriages,
people used to stay over all the time.

43:25.400 --> 43:26.920
But I have a car.

43:29.000 --> 43:30.120
Yes.

43:30.600 --> 43:31.760
And you have

43:33.000 --> 43:34.160
engine oil.

43:35.760 --> 43:36.880
Probably.

43:39.440 --> 43:41.360
But it would be very usual.

43:43.640 --> 43:44.640
What would?

43:46.840 --> 43:50.080
For a guest to stay. We have
a whole wing. A guest wing.

43:50.160 --> 43:51.360
Oh, good.

43:53.080 --> 43:54.520
You have a guest wing.

43:56.600 --> 43:58.400
Well... [exhales]

44:05.400 --> 44:06.600
May?

44:16.800 --> 44:18.160
Have a smoke.

44:18.240 --> 44:19.440
Thank you.

44:28.360 --> 44:30.560
Ignore this. It's actually all right.

44:33.640 --> 44:36.440
I put all his photos
in a drawer and locked it.

44:37.320 --> 44:39.760
As if that was going
to make it different.

44:43.120 --> 44:45.360
I am like a fucking lighthouse keeper.

44:49.520 --> 44:51.320
Out here on my own.

44:53.400 --> 44:55.400
Keeping his flame burning.

44:55.480 --> 44:56.920
But of course,

44:57.000 --> 44:58.960
when anyone sees a lighthouse

44:59.760 --> 45:01.520
they steer clear, don't they?

45:12.920 --> 45:14.520
Now, do you have a map?

45:17.960 --> 45:19.840
Of the house. Do you have a map?

45:22.720 --> 45:23.920
Because

45:24.920 --> 45:27.960
I'm not going to be able
to find me way in the dark.

45:31.240 --> 45:32.600
You see, at midnight,

45:34.280 --> 45:36.520
I'm going to leave my wing.

45:37.840 --> 45:39.840
And I'm going to come
and find you.

45:40.680 --> 45:43.840
And I' m going to turn the handle
of your bedroom door

45:43.920 --> 45:45.320
without making a sound.

45:46.120 --> 45:48.000
And none of the maids will know.

45:49.480 --> 45:50.760
You don't know maids.

45:53.360 --> 45:54.880
They hear a pin drop.

45:56.080 --> 45:58.640
If there is a man in the house,
they listen.

45:59.840 --> 46:01.520
They take turns.

46:02.120 --> 46:03.600
So let them listen.

46:08.960 --> 46:10.040
Right.

46:10.120 --> 46:11.920
Day rate, seven shillings.

46:12.000 --> 46:14.360
Loading unloading.
Night rate, three.

46:14.560 --> 46:15.960
Total ten.

46:16.560 --> 46:18.040
Let's go and get a drink.

46:18.480 --> 46:19.840
No, I'm all right.

46:19.920 --> 46:21.280
Come on, it's pay day.

46:21.360 --> 46:24.440
- Everybody has a drink on pay day.
- Not me!

46:24.600 --> 46:27.760
You think your mom don't have
a drink on pay day?

46:27.920 --> 46:30.200
You think the Lord Almighty

46:30.520 --> 46:32.360
don't have a drink on pay day?

46:33.760 --> 46:35.440
Man, it's on fire out there.

46:35.520 --> 46:37.400
Women from the BSA offices.

46:37.480 --> 46:38.520
Honest.

46:38.680 --> 46:41.480
They go out on their own
without men these days.

46:41.560 --> 46:43.240
In twos and threes now.

46:44.360 --> 46:48.120
Mickey, they go out in twos and threes
and buy their own drinks.

46:52.880 --> 46:54.160
The Marquis.

46:54.240 --> 46:57.120
That's where the BSA secretaries go.

47:01.280 --> 47:02.400
Look.

47:02.720 --> 47:06.120
They won't serve a black man
without a Shelby by his side.

47:11.080 --> 47:12.400
So you're using me.

47:15.640 --> 47:17.920
Yeah. Come on.

47:23.160 --> 47:26.360
- [people talking and laughing]
- [swing music playing]

47:26.440 --> 47:29.640
I told you. I fucking told you.
This city is going mad.

47:29.720 --> 47:31.520
Girls go out on their own now.

47:34.440 --> 47:36.360
Two pints of dark mild, please.

47:36.440 --> 47:39.200
Fuck that. We're Peaky boys.
Two whiskeys.

47:48.240 --> 47:50.560
You get used to it.
Then you can't stop.

47:54.440 --> 47:55.720
[man whistles]

47:57.000 --> 47:58.560
I don't drink with blacks.

47:59.520 --> 48:00.920
Here we fucking go.

48:01.000 --> 48:03.360
Paddy, they're okay.

48:03.920 --> 48:05.360
Not with me they're not.

48:05.440 --> 48:08.280
- I don't drink in pubs with blacks.
- So leave.

48:08.800 --> 48:11.120
Look, my dad is
the preacher round here.

48:11.200 --> 48:13.840
- He has connections. Ask anybody.
- [scoffs]

48:13.920 --> 48:15.840
I couldn't give
a fuck who he is.

48:15.920 --> 48:17.920
I'm telling you
to leave it, Paddy.

48:18.000 --> 48:20.760
And I'm telling this darkie
to get out of here.

48:20.840 --> 48:24.760
- You know something? Fuck off.
- It's all right, Isaiah, leave it.

48:24.840 --> 48:27.640
- We'll go to my cousin's pub.
- I like it here.

48:29.040 --> 48:30.840
You fucking nigger.

48:30.920 --> 48:33.440
- [boys shouting]
- [people screaming]

48:52.080 --> 48:53.240
[bartender] Paddy!

48:53.320 --> 48:55.240
The white kid's a Peaky Blinder!

48:55.320 --> 48:56.920
He's Polly Shelby's son.

48:58.440 --> 49:00.440
He's Polly Shelby's son.

49:00.520 --> 49:02.520
Are you fucking suicidal?

49:02.600 --> 49:05.520
Sir? Sir, I'm sorry.
I didn't know.

49:05.600 --> 49:07.360
- Get out!
- I'm sorry.

49:07.440 --> 49:10.360
- Get out the fucking pub.
- I really didn't know.

49:10.440 --> 49:12.320
Fuck! Let's go! Let's go!

49:15.160 --> 49:16.840
We ordered some drinks?

49:17.200 --> 49:19.000
And we are going
to drink them.

49:29.840 --> 49:32.600
His face, man.
Did you see his fucking face?

49:32.680 --> 49:34.640
You punch like
a fucking kangaroo.

49:34.720 --> 49:37.560
I seen one once in a circus.
You punch like that.

49:38.280 --> 49:40.760
All right, anyway,
I need a fucking drink.

49:40.840 --> 49:44.160
- The drinks are on you!
- It's on me? You just got paid!

49:45.160 --> 49:46.640
And make it a whiskey.

49:50.680 --> 49:52.400
[people chattering]

49:52.480 --> 49:55.200
Fucking hell, it's packed
in here, isn't it?

49:55.520 --> 49:58.760
So it's as easy as that?
It's as easy as that, John boy.

49:58.840 --> 50:00.760
London. You wouldn't believe it.

50:00.840 --> 50:03.280
- Finn, stick your head in a bucket.
- No.

50:03.960 --> 50:06.800
The women, John boy, the women.

50:06.880 --> 50:09.320
There is this one,
she's a dancer, right?

50:09.400 --> 50:11.280
She can do things with her...

50:13.880 --> 50:15.480
If Finn would
just piss off,

50:15.560 --> 50:18.040
- I'd explain a little bit better.
- Yeah.

50:18.120 --> 50:21.960
- Well, if it's so good, why are you here?
- A decent pint.

50:22.040 --> 50:24.080
The cockneys can't get that right.

50:24.160 --> 50:25.360
He misses us, Finn.

50:25.440 --> 50:27.280
That's what it is.
[door opens]

50:27.360 --> 50:29.200
[Arthur] Here they are, look.

50:29.280 --> 50:31.000
Junior Peaky boys.

50:33.320 --> 50:36.120
What's happened to you,
have you been scrapping?

50:36.200 --> 50:38.760
- Oh.
- Some idiots at the Marquis of Lorne.

50:38.840 --> 50:42.720
They tried to stop me and Isiah
from drinking but it is all right.

50:42.800 --> 50:44.080
We fought them off.

50:44.160 --> 50:46.840
Arthur, we had to stand
our ground and we did.

50:46.920 --> 50:48.480
The Marquis, eh?

50:48.560 --> 50:49.840
Where's your dad?

50:51.000 --> 50:52.600
- Preaching.
- Right.

50:55.680 --> 50:57.720
Don't nick any of me chips, Finn!

50:57.800 --> 51:00.240
- Where are you going?
- We won't be long.

51:01.320 --> 51:02.360
[door closes]

51:02.440 --> 51:03.720
Where are they going?

51:04.000 --> 51:05.400
The Marquis of Lorne.

51:06.360 --> 51:07.480
Shame.

51:08.120 --> 51:09.360
It was a nice pub.

51:09.920 --> 51:11.560
[erotic music playing]

51:24.240 --> 51:25.400
[door closes]

52:59.480 --> 53:01.400
[breathing heavily]

53:01.480 --> 53:04.480
Ah! Ah! Ah!

53:05.240 --> 53:08.240
Looks like the big bastard got cut.

53:10.360 --> 53:12.320
You didn't have
to do that for me!

53:12.400 --> 53:14.280
We didn't do it
for you, Michael.

53:14.360 --> 53:16.840
We did it for the good name
of the company.

53:17.480 --> 53:19.400
None of this happened, all right?

53:19.960 --> 53:22.480
Come on. Let's go
and get that drink.

53:31.640 --> 53:33.200
[door opens]

53:33.520 --> 53:36.800
[footsteps approaching]

53:45.920 --> 53:48.880
[breathes deeply]

53:49.920 --> 53:51.640
The date has been set.

53:53.240 --> 53:55.520
Three weeks from today.

53:58.840 --> 54:00.360
You checked out his house?

54:02.280 --> 54:04.360
There's coppers all over it.

54:05.200 --> 54:06.960
You'll take care of them?

54:07.520 --> 54:09.920
No. It would arouse suspicion.

54:11.040 --> 54:13.280
You'll have to deal
with them yourself.

54:15.600 --> 54:17.600
And if a copper gets killed?

54:19.640 --> 54:21.880
There will be no
consequences for you.

54:21.960 --> 54:23.560
I have given you my word.

54:23.760 --> 54:25.480
Which I know
to be worthless.

54:27.520 --> 54:30.480
That is why I have
been settling my affairs

54:30.560 --> 54:32.200
in the event of my death.

54:35.360 --> 54:38.520
[chuckling] And making your peace
with God, perhaps?

54:40.640 --> 54:42.520
Good luck with that.

54:47.400 --> 54:49.200
I have made arrangements.

54:49.800 --> 54:51.520
With men I trust.

54:53.080 --> 54:54.800
If I should die,

54:55.400 --> 54:57.000
then you will die.

54:58.480 --> 54:59.960
Know that.

55:04.120 --> 55:07.320
[footsteps retreating]

55:39.320 --> 55:40.800
[exhales heavily]

56:33.040 --> 56:34.040
Ritz Hotel.

56:34.120 --> 56:35.320
[clears throat]

56:35.600 --> 56:36.760
London, Piccadilly.

56:36.840 --> 56:41.280
[ringing]

56:46.560 --> 56:47.680
Hello?

56:49.360 --> 56:50.520
Hello?

56:52.640 --> 56:53.920
[hangs up phone]

56:56.960 --> 56:58.920
- Who was that?
- Call got cut.

56:59.000 --> 57:02.480
I guess the telephone lines in
England are not so good, huh?

57:10.080 --> 57:13.000
[theme song playing]
